"""CS331 Chapter 7 (network layer data transfer: switching, IPv4 addressing, subnetting, DHCP, ARP, IPv4 header, NAT).

Explaining notes and explained questions, by slide. NOTES replace a slide's plain notes in order (highlights link to
note numbers); extra notes join the last one. Q replaces the deck's old questions. All are predictions; every
subnetting answer was checked with Python's ipaddress module.
"""
from .common import pick, truth, written

NOTES = {
    1: ['The deck covers the network layer’s job of moving packets: its duties; the two ways of switching packets (**virtual circuit** and **datagram**); **IPv4 addresses** (notation, classes, network/host/broadcast addresses); **masks and subnetting**; how a host gets its settings (**DHCP**) and finds a neighbour’s MAC address (**ARP**); the **IPv4 header** and **fragmentation**; and **NAT**.',
        'Subnetting is the biggest calculation topic in the course, and it only becomes fast with practice. The whole method fits in four steps, shown in the tip below: work out the block size from the mask, find the multiple of the block at or below the address (the network), the next multiple minus one (the broadcast), and everything in between (the usable hosts). Every subnetting slide below works through this with numbers.'],
    2: ['Three sections: **7.1 services** (the duties of the network layer), **7.2 packet switching** (virtual circuit vs. datagram), and **7.4 Internet Protocol version 4** (addressing, subnetting, DHCP, ARP, the header, fragmentation, NAT). Section 7.4 is most of the deck and most of the exam weight.'],
    3: ['The network layer has five duties. **Internetworking**: joining different networks into one internet. **Addressing**: giving every interface a unique, global logical address (the IP address). **Routing**: finding the best path through the networks. **Packetizing**: wrapping the transport layer’s data in a packet. **Fragmenting**: cutting a packet into smaller pieces when the next network can’t carry it whole. Framing is not on the list: it belongs to the data link layer.'],
    4: ['**Packetizing** is the first duty: at the source, the network layer **encapsulates** the payload (a segment from the transport layer) in a packet; at the destination it **decapsulates** it and hands the payload up. Its promise is to **carry the payload from source to destination without changing it or using it**, exactly like a postal service delivering a parcel without opening it.'],
    5: ['**Routing** is the planning job: routers run **routing protocols** with each other to find the best paths, and store the results in a decision-making table called the **routing table**. It happens in the background, before and while packets flow.',
        '**Forwarding** is the action taken for each packet: when a packet arrives on one interface, the router looks up the destination in its table and decides **which interface to send it out of**. A comparison: routing is planning the trip on a map; forwarding is turning the right way at each junction.'],
    6: ['From routing and forwarding we can see that the network layer does a kind of **switching**. A router is, in fact, a **switch**: it connects one of its input ports to one (or more) of its output ports for each packet, just as an electrical switch connects an input to an output so current can flow.'],
    7: ['There are two approaches to packet switching. The **virtual-circuit** approach is **connection-oriented**: a path is set up first, and all packets of a message follow it. The **datagram** approach is **connectionless**: every packet is handled on its own and may take its own path. The Internet uses the datagram approach.'],
    8: ['In a **connection-oriented** service, the **virtual-circuit approach**, all packets of a message are related: they belong to one connection. Before any are sent, a **virtual connection** is set up that fixes the path. After setup, all packets follow the **same path**, so they arrive in order. Each packet carries the source and destination addresses and also a **flow label**, a **virtual circuit identifier** that tells each switch which virtual path the packet belongs to.'],
    9: ['During setup, a **connect request** travels switch by switch (SW1, SW2 … SWn) to the destination, and each switch reserves what the connection needs: **buffer space, bandwidth, delay limits**. A **connect confirm** comes back, and data can flow. Why is it called **virtual**? Because the switches, buffers and lines are **shared** by packets of many connections; nothing is physically dedicated to one connection, unlike a real telephone circuit.'],
    10: ['All packets of the message follow the same virtual circuit, here through R1, R3 and R4, so they arrive **in order** (1, 2, 3, 4), and no router has to make a fresh routing decision for each one.'],
    11: ['How a packet follows its circuit: each switch keeps a table that maps **(incoming port, incoming label) → (outgoing port, outgoing label)**. The packet arrives with label 14; the switch looks it up, swaps the label to 66 and sends it out of the stored port; the next switch swaps 66 for 22, and so on. Labels only have meaning between two neighbouring switches, so they can be short.'],
    12: ['Delay in a circuit-switched or virtual-circuit network has three phases: **connect** (setup, the request going out and the confirm coming back), **data transfer**, and **disconnect** (teardown). Setup and teardown add delay before the first bit of data moves, but after that each packet just follows the established path with no per-packet routing decisions.'],
    13: ['When the Internet began, the network layer was designed, for simplicity, to give a **connectionless** service: each packet is handled **independently**, with no relationship to any other packet.',
         'The network layer is only responsible for delivering each packet from source to destination. Packets of the same message may or may not take the same path, and keeping them in order (if needed) is left to the transport layer.'],
    14: ['The one line to remember: **communication at the network layer in the Internet is connectionless**. IP is a datagram protocol: no setup, no reserved path, each packet routed on its own.'],
    15: ['A **connectionless** network has **no handshaking** (no setup phase). Each packet is sent and routed independently and may follow a different path, so **each packet must carry the full source and destination addresses**: there is no label linking it to a connection.',
         'Consequences: there is **no setup delay**; packets are **not guaranteed to arrive in the order sent** (in the figure, packet 3 overtakes packet 2); and it is **robust**: if a router crashes, only the packets inside it are lost, and later packets simply take another path. This is what the Internet uses.'],
    16: ['Delay in a datagram network is the transmission time plus the **waiting (queuing) time at each router**, with **no setup phase**. But each router must look up the destination for every packet, and different packets may wait different amounts of time.'],
    17: ['A connectionless router’s forwarding table maps **destination address → output interface**. When a packet arrives, the router reads only its **destination address**, finds it in the table, and sends the packet out of the matching interface (here, interface 2). The source address plays no part in the decision.'],
    18: ['The network-layer address in TCP/IP is the **IP address**. An IPv4 address is **32 bits**, written as **4 octets** (bytes).',
         'IP addresses are **unique** (each connection to the Internet has a different address) and **universal** (every host that wants to connect to the Internet must accept the same addressing system). To avoid conflicts they are managed by a non-profit organisation, **ICANN** (Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers). ICANN assigns blocks to **regional authorities**, which assign them to **ISPs**, which assign them to customers. ICANN also manages DNS (domain names).'],
    19: ['With 32 bits there are **2³² = 4,294,967,296** possible IPv4 addresses, about 4.3 billion. That sounds like a lot, but the world has far more devices than that, which is why classless addressing, private addresses with NAT, and IPv6 exist.'],
    20: ['The same address can be written two ways. **Binary**: 10000000 00001011 00000011 00011111. **Dotted decimal**: each 8-bit group converted to decimal and separated by dots: 128.11.3.31. Dotted decimal is what people use; binary is what you need for masks and subnetting.'],
    21: ['To convert binary to dotted decimal, convert each 8-bit group separately using the bit weights **128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1** and add the weights of the 1 bits. 10000001 = 128 + 1 = 129; 00001011 = 8 + 2 + 1 = 11; 11101111 = 128 + 64 + 32 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 239. So (a) is 129.11.11.239, and (b) is 249.155.251.15.'],
    22: ['To convert decimal to binary, go through the weights 128, 64, 32 … 1: if the weight fits into what is left, write 1 and subtract it; otherwise write 0. 111: 128 doesn’t fit (0); 64 fits (1, leaves 47); 32 fits (1, leaves 15); 16 doesn’t (0); 8 fits (1, leaves 7); 4 (1, leaves 3); 2 (1, leaves 1); 1 (1). So 111 = **01101111**. Always write 8 bits per octet, with leading zeros.'],
    23: ['Each octet is 8 bits, so it must be between **0 and 255**. 75.45.301.14 is invalid because 301 > 255. Other errors to look for: more or fewer than four octets (1.2.3.4.5), a mix of binary and decimal, and leading zeros such as 045 (not allowed in dotted decimal).'],
    24: ['An IP address has two parts. The **prefix** (the first n bits) identifies the **network** the host is on; the **suffix** (the remaining 32 - n bits) identifies the **connection** of the host to that network. Setting the suffix to all 0s gives the **network address** (also called network or block ID). The suffix is the **host ID**, but strictly it identifies the host’s **interface** (its connection), not the machine itself.'],
    25: ['An IP address belongs to an **interface**, not to a machine. A router connected to three networks has **three interfaces**, and so **three IP addresses**, each one from the network that interface is on. A laptop with both Wi-Fi and Ethernet connected likewise has two IP addresses.'],
    26: ['In the original **classful** addressing, the address space was divided into five classes: **A, B, C, D and E**. The class decided how many bits were network and how many host, so it decided how big each network was.'],
    27: ['How the address space was shared out: **class A** took half of all addresses (**50%**), **class B** a quarter (**25%**), **class C** an eighth (**12.5%**), and **classes D and E 6.25%** each. Class A had very few, huge blocks, which wasted many addresses: one of the reasons classful addressing was abandoned.'],
    28: ['The class can be read from the **first bits**: **A** starts with **0**, **B** with **10**, **C** with **110**, **D** with **1110**, **E** with **1111**.',
         'Or from the **first byte** in decimal: **A 0–127**, **B 128–191**, **C 192–223**, **D 224–239**, **E 240–255**. Remember the upper limits 127, 191, 223, 239, 255 and you can classify any address instantly.'],
    29: ['**Class A**: first bit 0, first byte 0–127. **8 network bits** and **24 host bits**, so each block has 2²⁴ = 16,777,216 addresses, minus 2 usable (the all-0s host part is the **network address** x.0.0.0 and the all-1s host part is the **broadcast address** x.255.255.255), giving 16,777,214 hosts. The valid blocks are 1 to 126 (126 blocks): **0.x.x.x is reserved** (0.0.0.0 means the default route, or a device that doesn’t know its own address yet), and **127.x.x.x is reserved for loopback** (a host sending to itself, for testing, as in ping 127.0.0.1). All class A blocks are allocated (large networks).',
         '**Class B**: first bits 10, first byte 128–191. 2 fixed bits + 14 network bits give **2¹⁴ = 16,384 blocks**; 16 host bits give **2¹⁶ = 65,536** addresses per block, **65,534** usable. All allocated (medium networks).'],
    30: ['**Class C**: first bits 110, first byte 192–223. 3 fixed bits + 21 network bits give **2²¹ = 2,097,152 blocks**; 8 host bits give **256** addresses per block, **254** usable. Nearly all allocated (small networks).',
         '**Class D** is for **multicast**: one sender to a specific group of receivers. Its first four bits are 1110, and it has **no network/host split**: the whole address names a group.',
         '**Private IP addresses** are used inside local networks and are **not routable** on the public Internet: **10.0.0.0–10.255.255.255**, **172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255** and **192.168.0.0–192.168.255.255**. Anyone may use them internally; to reach the Internet, they are translated by NAT (slide 82 onward). Watch the 172 range: 172.16 to 172.31 only, so 172.32.x.x is public.'],
    31: ['The figure shows a sample internet mixing networks of different classes (A, B and C), joined by routers and WAN links. Each network has its own network address, and each router interface takes an address from the network it is on.'],
    32: ['Summary of the classes: **A** (0, large networks), **B** (10, medium networks), **C** (110, small networks), **D** (1110, multicast), **E** (1111, reserved for experimental use).'],
    33: ['Classifying in binary: (a) 00000001 … starts with **0**, so class **A**. (b) 11110011 … starts with **1111**, so class **E**. Read only as many leading bits as you need: stop at the first 0.'],
    34: ['Classifying in decimal, by the first byte: (a) **227** is between 224 and 239, so class **D**. (b) **252** is between 240 and 255, so class **E**. (c) **134** is between 128 and 191, so class **B**.'],
    35: ['Finding the classful network address: work out the class, keep the network bytes and set the host bytes to 0. 23.56.7.91 is class **A** (first byte 23), so only the first byte is network: the network address is **23.0.0.0**.'],
    36: ['132.6.17.85 is class **B** (first byte 132), so the first **two** bytes are network: the network address is **132.6.0.0**. For class C, keep three bytes: 201.14.78.65 → 201.14.78.0.'],
    37: ['Going the other way: given the network address 17.0.0.0, the netid is only **one byte**, so it is class **A** (which also matches 17 being in 0–127).'],
    38: ['Every network has **three kinds** of address. The **network address**: host part all 0s, for example 192.168.10.0; it names the network and can’t be given to a host.',
         '**Host addresses** are everything between: here 192.168.10.1 (the first usable) to 192.168.10.254 (the last usable). The **directed broadcast** address has the host part all 1s, 192.168.10.255; a packet sent to it reaches every host on that network.'],
    39: ['A **directed broadcast** address is used to send a message to **all nodes of a specific network**, usually a network **different** from the sender’s. It is the network ID with **all 1s in the host part** (192.168.10.255 for 192.168.10.0/24). **Routers forward it** towards that network. Because attackers have used it to flood networks (denial-of-service attacks), it is usually blocked.'],
    40: ['A **limited broadcast** address is for a message to **all nodes on the sender’s own network**. It is **255.255.255.255**, and **routers never forward it**, because routers separate broadcast domains. DHCP uses it, for example, when a new host doesn’t yet know its own network. The trap: the directed broadcast (192.168.10.255) can cross routers; the limited broadcast (255.255.255.255) cannot.'],
    41: ['In classful addressing, what an organisation is given is a **network address**, which stands for the whole block: for example 132.6.0.0 gives it every address from 132.6.0.0 to 132.6.255.255.'],
    42: ['Questions the slide answers. **Why a mask?** To calculate the network (subnet) address of any IP address: the mask marks which bits are network.',
         '**Default mask vs. subnet mask**: the default (class) mask belongs to classful addressing, where the class decides the network size. A subnet mask belongs to classless addressing, where the **mask** decides it.',
         '**Valid lengths**: a subnet mask must be **longer than the default mask** of the address’s class (it borrows host bits), and no longer than **/30** (a /31 or /32 would leave no usable hosts, since 2 addresses are always lost to network and broadcast).',
         '**How to use it**: do a bitwise **AND** of the address and the mask. Shortcut, octet by octet: where the mask is **255**, copy the address octet; where it is **0**, write 0; for the one octet in between, convert both to binary, AND them, and convert back.'],
    43: ['Case 1, a host sending. The network layer needs the destination’s network address to decide: is the destination on the **same network** as me, or another one? It ANDs the destination address with its own mask and compares the result with its own network address.',
         'If it is the **same network**, the packet is handed to the data link layer to deliver **directly** to the destination (using the destination’s MAC).',
         'If it is **another network**, the data link layer is asked to send the frame to a **router**, the **default gateway** (the router interface on the sender’s own network), which will forward it. Example: 192.168.10.10/24 sending to 192.168.20.5: 192.168.10.0 ≠ 192.168.20.0, so it goes to the gateway.'],
    44: ['Case 2, a router forwarding. Routing tables are mostly organised by **network addresses**, not by individual host addresses (one entry covers a whole network).',
         'So the router ANDs the destination address with each entry’s mask to get the destination’s network address, finds the matching entry, and sends the packet to the **next hop** listed there.'],
    45: ['**Classful addressing isn’t used any more.** Today addresses are given out with **classless addressing** (CIDR), where classes play no part: the block size is set by the mask, written as **/n**.',
         'This was the answer to the huge demand for addresses and the shortage of IPv4. Blocks can now be any size, so addresses are distributed more carefully: ISPs receive large ranges and **subdivide** them into smaller blocks for their customers. Dividing a block into smaller networks is called **subnetting**.',
         'In classless addressing the mask is called the **subnet mask** and the network address the **subnet address**. Notation: byte.byte.byte.byte/n, where n (the prefix length) is the number of network bits, for example 220.8.24.255/25.'],
    46: ['The default masks of the classes: **A = 255.0.0.0 = /8**, **B = 255.255.0.0 = /16**, **C = 255.255.255.0 = /24**.',
         'A mask has **1s for every network bit** and **0s for every host bit**, with all the 1s first. The slash number just **counts the 1s**: /16 means the first 16 bits are network.'],
    47: ['To turn /n into dotted decimal, fill whole octets with 255 (8 bits each) and put the remaining bits at the start of the next octet. /12 = 8 + 4: 255, then 4 ones = 11110000 = 240 → **255.240.0.0**. /18 = 16 + 2 → **255.255.192.0**. /26 = 24 + 2 → **255.255.255.192**. /30 = 24 + 6 → **255.255.255.252**. Going backwards, count the 1s: 255.255.255.248 = 24 + 5 = /29.'],
    48: ['The only values a mask octet can take: 1 one = **128**, 2 = **192**, 3 = **224**, 4 = **240**, 5 = **248**, 6 = **252**, 7 = **254**, 8 = **255**. Learn this list; then the **block size** for an octet is simply 256 minus the value: 192 → 64, 224 → 32, 240 → 16, 248 → 8, 252 → 4.'],
    49: ['**AND** gives 1 only when both bits are 1: 1 AND 1 = 1, and every other combination is 0. ANDing the host address with the mask, bit by bit, keeps the network bits (where the mask has 1s) and clears the host bits (where it has 0s), which leaves exactly the subnet address. Example: 192.168.10.10 AND 255.255.255.0 = 192.168.10.0.'],
    50: ['172.16.200.100/19. The mask /19 = 255.255.224.0. The first two octets have mask 255, so copy them: 172.16. The last has mask 0, so write 0. Only the third octet needs binary: 200 = 11001000, 224 = 11100000, AND = 11000000 = **192**. Subnet address: **172.16.192.0**. Shortcut with block size: 256 - 224 = 32; the multiple of 32 at or below 200 is 192.'],
    51: ['193.1.2.129/26. The mask /26 = 255.255.255.192, so only the last octet needs work: 129 = 10000001, 192 = 11000000, AND = 10000000 = **128**. Subnet address: **193.1.2.128**. Block-size check: 256 - 192 = 64; multiples of 64 are 0, 64, 128, 192; 129 falls in the block starting at 128.'],
    52: ['172.16.200.200/18: the mask is 255.255.192.0, block size 64 in the third octet; the multiple of 64 at or below 200 is 192, so the subnet is **172.16.192.0**. (In binary: 200 AND 192 = 192.)',
         '220.1.2.162 with mask 255.255.255.252 (/30): block size 4 in the last octet; the multiple of 4 at or below 162 is 160, so the subnet is **220.1.2.160** (its broadcast is .163, leaving .161 and .162 for hosts).'],
    53: ['The motivating picture: one organisation (a campus) has one block of addresses but many departments, each with its own LAN (Art, English, French, Music, CS, EE, Math, Physics), all connected to a main router. Giving each department its own piece of the block is **subnetting**.'],
    54: ['The benefits of subnetting: **less overall traffic** (each subnet is its own broadcast domain, so broadcasts stay local); **smaller networks are easier to manage** and troubleshoot; **more control over the address space**; and **more security**, since traffic between subnets passes through a router where it can be filtered.'],
    55: ['**Subnetting** divides a network into smaller groups, **subnets**, each with its own subnet address. To the rest of the Internet, the site still looks like a **single network**: outside routers route by the main network address, and only the site’s own routers look at the subnet part.',
         'The **host part** of the address is split in two: the most significant bits become the **subnet number** and the rest stay the **host number**. So the address now has **three levels**: main network, subnet, host. The **subnet mask** marks which bits are subnet and which are host, and **ANDing** it with an address gives the subnet address.',
         'The figure: 172.16.0.0/22 has 22 network bits and 10 host bits, so each subnet has 2¹⁰ - 2 = **1022** usable addresses.'],
    56: ['The steps, with the example 192.168.1.0/24 into 5 subnets. (1) 5 - 1 = 4. (2) 4 in binary is 100. (3) That is **3 bits**. (4) Add them to the default mask: /24 + 3 = **/27** = 255.255.255.224. A quicker way to get the bits: the smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ the number of subnets (2³ = 8 ≥ 5).',
         '(5–6) The weight is the value of the last 1 bit of the mask in its octet: 224 = 11100000, the last 1 is worth **32** (also 256 - 224). (7–10) The first subnet is the original address, 192.168.1.0/27; keep adding 32: .32, .64, .96, .128, .160, .192, .224, and stop before passing 255. That gives 8 subnets of 32 addresses each.'],
    57: ['Given a classful network and a subnet mask: **number of subnets = 2^(subnet mask length - classful mask length)**, the number of bits borrowed. **Hosts per subnet = 2^(32 - subnet mask length) - 2**, subtracting the network and broadcast addresses, which can’t be given to hosts. Example: class B (/16) with /20 → 2⁴ = **16 subnets**, each with 2¹² - 2 = **4094 hosts**.'],
    58: ['The slide’s table is blank in the PDF; these are the answers for 192.168.1.0/24 split into **/27** subnets (block of 32 addresses, 30 usable). For each subnet: network = the multiple of 32, first usable = network + 1, broadcast = next network - 1, last usable = broadcast - 1. So .0 (hosts .1–.30, broadcast .31), .32 (.33–.62, .63), .64 (.65–.94, .95), .96 (.97–.126, .127), .128 (.129–.158, .159), .160 (.161–.190, .191), .192 (.193–.222, .223), .224 (.225–.254, .255).'],
    59: ['Using the subnets: a five-floor building gets one /27 per floor, all connected to router R1: 192.168.1.128/27, .96/27, .64/27, .32/27 and .0/27. The remaining three (.160, .192, .224) are kept for future expansion. Each floor can have up to 30 hosts, and each router interface takes one address (usually the first usable) from its floor’s subnet.'],
    60: ['Another blank practice table in the PDF: fill it in with the method from slides 56–58, and check against the answers listed for slide 58.'],
    61: ['141.14.0.0/16 (class B) is split with mask **/18** inside the site: 2 borrowed bits give **4 subnets**, with a block of 64 in the third octet: **141.14.0.0**, **141.14.64.0**, **141.14.128.0** and **141.14.192.0**. Routers R1 to R3 inside the site use 255.255.192.0; the outside world still sees just 141.14.0.0/16.'],
    62: ['Once an organisation has a block from its ISP, it assigns addresses to its hosts and router interfaces. Every host needs **four settings**: an **IP address** from the correct subnet; the **subnet mask**; the **default gateway** (the router interface on the host’s own subnet, used for anything outside it); and the **DNS server** address (to turn names into IP addresses).',
         'These can be configured **manually**, by the administrator typing them into a configuration file. But that fails for a **diskless** computer (nowhere to store the settings), a computer **booting for the first time**, or one that **moves to another subnet** (its old settings are wrong there). The solution is **DHCP**, the Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol.'],
    63: ['The example network: one router joins two networks. **192.168.10.0/24** holds hosts A to D, and the router’s interface there is **192.168.10.1**. **192.168.20.0/24** holds host S, and the router’s interface there is **192.168.20.1**. The next slide asks questions about a packet from A to S.'],
    64: ['**Default gateways**: for A, B, C and D it is the router interface on their own network, **192.168.10.1**. For S it is **192.168.20.1**. A default gateway is always on the host’s own subnet.',
         '**IP addresses**: when A sends to S, the packet’s source IP is **A** and destination IP is **S**, and they are the **same** in the packet S receives. Routers don’t change IP addresses (without NAT).',
         '**MAC addresses**: the frame leaving A has source = **A’s MAC** and destination = the MAC of the **router’s 192.168.10.1 interface** (the next hop, found with ARP). The frame S receives has source = the MAC of the **router’s 192.168.20.1 interface** and destination = **S’s MAC**. MAC addresses change at every hop; IP addresses don’t.'],
    65: ['211.56.77.35/29: the mask /29 = 255.255.255.248, block size **8**. The multiple of 8 at or below 35 is 32, so the subnet is 211.56.77.32 and the next one starts at 40. The **directed broadcast** is the next network minus one: **211.56.77.39** (usable hosts .33 to .38).',
         'The other two are the same as slide 52: 172.16.200.200/18 → **172.16.192.0**, and 220.1.2.162/30 → **220.1.2.160**.'],
    66: ['**DHCP** is an **application-layer** protocol (it runs over UDP) that gives a host, including a diskless computer, its **IP address, subnet mask, default gateway and DNS server** automatically. It is an application protocol even though the settings it hands out belong to the network layer, which is a common trap.',
         'The DHCP server keeps **two databases**. The **static** database maps **MAC addresses to permanent IP addresses** (used for diskless workstations and servers that must always have the same address). The **dynamic** database is a **pool** of unused addresses handed out temporarily.',
         'When a host asks, the server checks the **static** database first. If the host’s MAC isn’t there, it takes a free address from the **dynamic pool** and gives it as a **temporary** address with a **lease**: the address is the host’s only for a set **period of time**, so unused addresses aren’t wasted. When the lease expires, the host must **return** the address or **renew** the lease.'],
    67: ['A node about to send a packet knows the **IP address** of the next node (the destination itself, or the default gateway). But to build a **frame** for the link, it needs that node’s **link-layer (MAC) address**: the IP address alone can’t move a frame across a link. The **Address Resolution Protocol (ARP)** finds the MAC address that goes with an IP address.'],
    68: ['**ARP** is a **network-layer** protocol (drawn beside IP, ICMP and IGMP) that translates an **IP address** into the matching **MAC (layer-2) address**. Direction matters: IP → MAC. (DNS is the one that turns names into IP addresses.)'],
    69: ['An **ARP request is broadcast**: the sender doesn’t know who owns the IP address, so it asks everyone on the LAN, “Who has IP address N2? Tell me your MAC.” An **ARP reply is unicast**: only the owner answers, directly to the asker, “N2 is me; my MAC is L2.” Memory aid: ask everyone, answer one.'],
    70: ['The figure: (a) system A **broadcasts** a request asking for the link-layer address of the host with IP N2; every host receives it. (b) Only system B, which owns N2, **replies by unicast** with its link-layer address L2. A then stores the answer in its ARP cache so it doesn’t have to ask again for a while.'],
    71: ['An ARP packet is carried **directly in an Ethernet frame**, with the frame’s type field set to **0x0806**, not inside an IP packet. That is why ARP only works within one local network: the request is a link-layer broadcast, which routers don’t pass on.'],
    72: ['An IPv4 datagram is a **header of 20 to 60 bytes** followed by the payload, up to **65,535 bytes** in total. The header fields: VER (version), HLEN (header length), service type, total length, identification, flags, fragmentation offset, time to live, protocol, header checksum, source address, destination address, and options.'],
    73: ['The header is at least **20 bytes** (without options) and at most 60. The **total length** field covers header + data, up to **65,535** bytes.',
         '**Identification**, **flags** and **fragment offset** are used for **fragmentation and reassembly**. A packet can be **fragmented at any node** between the source and the destination, but **reassembly happens only at the destination**, because fragments may take different routes and only the destination is sure to receive them all.',
         '**Time to live (TTL)** stops lost packets from circling between routers forever (in a routing loop). The source sets it to a starting value (depending on its operating system); **each router subtracts 1** and checks it, and if it reaches **0** the packet is **dropped**. The **protocol** field holds a code saying what is carried in the data (TCP, UDP, a routing protocol), so the destination knows whom to hand it to.',
         'The **header checksum** checks for errors in the **header only**, not the data (the transport layer checks the data). It is **recomputed at every router**, because the header changes on each hop: at least the TTL is decremented.'],
    74: ['The **protocol** field says which upper protocol the payload belongs to. The source writes the code and the destination uses it to deliver the data to the right protocol. Values to know: **ICMP 1**, **IGMP 2**, **TCP 6**, **UDP 17**, **OSPF 89**.'],
    75: ['A datagram crosses many different networks. At each router it is taken out of the incoming frame (decapsulated), processed, and put into a new frame for the next network (encapsulated). The format and size of that frame depend on the next network: a router joining a LAN to a WAN receives LAN frames and sends WAN frames. If the next network’s frames are smaller than the datagram, the datagram has to be cut up.'],
    76: ['Each network has its own **MTU** (maximum transfer unit, the largest payload its frame can carry): Hyperchannel 65,535; Token Ring 17,914 (16 Mbps) and 4,464 (4 Mbps); FDDI 4,352; **Ethernet 1,500**; X.25 576; PPP 296. Ethernet’s MTU of 1500 bytes is the same number as Ethernet’s maximum data field from Chapter 4.'],
    77: ['Every link-layer protocol has its own frame format, and one of its properties is the **MTU**: the maximum size of data that can be put in one frame.',
         'So the network-layer packet must be **no larger than the MTU** of the network it is about to cross. If it is larger, it must be **fragmented** into pieces that fit.'],
    78: ['The same point from the router’s side: each router re-encapsulates the datagram in the frame type of the next network. If the next network’s MTU is smaller than the datagram, the router fragments it before sending.'],
    79: ['**Fragmentation** can take place at the **sender** and at **any router**. **Reassembly** takes place **only at the final receiver**. A fragment can even be fragmented again by a later router with a smaller MTU; each fragment travels as an independent datagram with its own header.'],
    80: ['The **flags** field has three bits: reserved, **D** (do not fragment) and **M** (more fragments). **M = 1** means this is **not the last** fragment; **M = 0** means it is the **last** one (or the packet was never fragmented). **D = 1** means the packet **must not be fragmented**: if such a packet reaches a router whose next link has a smaller MTU, the router **drops** it (and reports an error with ICMP).'],
    81: ['The header checksum is the same **one’s-complement checksum** as in Chapter 3, calculated over the **header only**: the header is split into 16-bit words, they are added with wrap-around, and the sum is complemented (giving CBB0 in the example). If a router finds an error, it **drops** the packet. It is recomputed at **every router**, because fields such as the TTL change at every hop.'],
    82: ['**NAT** (Network Address Translation) maps between **private (local)** and **public (global)** IP addresses. It lets a whole site use **private addresses internally** and only a few public addresses (at least one) to communicate with the rest of the world.'],
    83: ['Why NAT is needed: business customers and many home users stay **connected all the time**, so each needs an address all the time, and an ISP doesn’t have enough addresses for all its customers (a class B block supports only 65,536).',
         'The solution is a **NAT-enabled router** between the site and the ISP: everyone inside uses private addresses, and the router translates them to its own public address on the way out.'],
    84: ['The private ranges again: **10.0.0.0/8**, **172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255**, **192.168.0.0/16**. Inside the site in the figure, hosts use private addresses **172.18.3.1 to 172.18.3.20**. The NAT router’s outside interface has the one **public address** given by the ISP, **200.24.5.8**. To the Internet, the whole site looks like that one address.'],
    85: ['How the translation works. For an **outgoing** packet, the NAT router replaces the **source** address: private **172.18.3.1** becomes public **200.24.5.8**. For an **incoming** reply, it replaces the **destination** address: **200.24.5.8** becomes **172.18.3.1** again. Out → change the source; in → change the destination.'],
    86: ['How does the router know which inside host a reply is for? When the outgoing packet passes, it records an entry in a **translation table** (private address 172.18.3.1 ↔ destination 25.8.2.10). When a reply comes from 25.8.2.10, the router finds the entry and changes the destination back to 172.18.3.1.'],
    87: ['In practice: the company connects to its ISP through a NAT-enabled router that has the one unique public address. The router keeps a **translation table** (up to 65,536 entries), and each row has **four fields**: **private source address**, **source port number**, **destination address**, and a **NAT port number** that must be unique. The port numbers are what let many inside hosts share one public address: each connection gets its own NAT port, so replies can be told apart. Every machine inside has a unique **private address**, usually from 10.x.y.z. In the basic version shown, NAT won’t let more than one inside host contact the **same** destination at the same time, since the replies couldn’t be told apart.'],
    88: ['Advantages of NAT: **saves IP addresses** (one public address for all the site’s devices, so no need for a range from the ISP); internal addresses can be **changed without telling the outside world**; the site can **change ISP** without renumbering its devices; and it acts like a **firewall**: an outside computer **can’t start a connection** to an inside one, because there is no table entry until the inside computer makes contact (and Internet routers won’t forward packets addressed to private IPs). You can browse and download, but nobody outside can open a connection to a port on your machine.'],
}

